Power one monitor off and on by name

niri powers monitors off only all at once, and any key or pointer
motion lights them all again. A laptop kept working beside a monitor
in the bedroom had no way to darken the monitor alone, and turning
the monitor off at its button cuts the USB hub and network card it
carries.

power-off-monitor and power-on-monitor name one output, as
focus-monitor does: by connector, or by make, model and serial. Off,
the output's CRTC is inactive, as power-off-monitors leaves every
one, so it stays connected and in the layout, its windows where they
were, and whatever hangs off its port keeps its power. It isn't drawn,
and doesn't keep the session from locking.

An output off by name stays off through keys and pointer motion while
another is lit; its own power-on-monitor, or power-on-monitors, lights
it. Once every output is off by name, input lights them all, as it does
after power-off-monitors. A reconnect, coming back from another VT or
a restart of niri lights it too. power-off-monitors and
power-on-monitors otherwise keep their behaviour, and input after
power-off-monitors leaves an output off by name dark.

niri msg outputs says which is off, as powered_off in its JSON, which
an older niri's answer reads as false. niri sets
NIRI_POWERS_OFF_EACH_OUTPUT=1 for its children and imports it into
the session, so a shell restarted under a niri without this patch
offers it only once niri restarts.

